/	MR8E-C ROM CONTENTS

/	SOURCE RECREATION BY:

/	CHARLES LASNER	P?S NY

/	NOTE: MUST BE ASSEMBLED WITH THE '/0' COMMAND-LINE SWITCH OFF.  [TO USE OS/8
/	PAL8 THE '/O' COMMAND-LINE SWITCH MUST BE SET.]

/	LAST EDIT:	14-FEB-2016	16:00:00	CJL
	XLIST	OFF			/TURN LISTING OFF NOW.

	IFNDEF	OFF	<OFF=	1>	/TURN LISTING OFF WITH THIS.

	IFNDEF	ON	<ON=	0>	/TURN LISTING ON WITH THIS.

	XLIST	ON			/TURN LISTING ON NOW.

/	THE MR8E-C ROM IS USED WITH THE PRIMARY TD8E CONTROLLER [DEVICE CODE 77] TO
/	ACHIEVE BOOTABLE OPERATING SYSTEMS REQUIRING MINIMAL READ/WRITE MEMORY.  THE
/	PDP-8 CONVENTION OF THE ENTIRE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER BEING CONTAINED IN THE
/	READ/WRITE MEMORY OF LOCATIONS 07600-07777 CAN BE REALIZED [PERHAPS WITH SOME
/	EFFORT].  DEPENDING ON THE PARTICULAR OPERATING SYSTEM, THIS COULD BE A
/	MANDATORY REQUIREMENT; ALTERNATIVELY, THE OPERATING SYSTEM MUST BE
/	RECONFIGURED TO HANDLE THE SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS IN SOME OTHER SYSTEM-DEPENDENT
/	MANNER [SUCH AS REQUIRING ADDITIONAL MEMORY] WHICH IMPACTS ON OTHER ASPECTS OF
/	THE OPERATING SYSTEM AND/OR MACHINE COSTS.  THE MR8E-C ROM ALLOWS A VIABLE
/	SOLUTION AS LONG AS THE DEVICE HANDLER CAN FIT INTO THE DESIGNATED CODE SPACE
/	OF 07600-07777 [WITH EACH SYSTEM RESERVING CERTAIN INTERNAL LOCATIONS THAT
/	INTERACT WITH THE ROM] WITHOUT UNDUE COMPROMISE.

/	SINCE PDP-8 MEMORY CONVENTIONS ARE SUCH THAT ONLY ENTIRE 4096 WORD READ/WRITE
/	MEMORY FIELDS ARE UTILIZED, THE FACT THAT A PORTION OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE
/	HANDLER IS ACTUALLY IMPLEMENTED AS A ROM IS UNDETECTABLE TO THE REST OF THE
/	OPERATING SYSTEM; THE ROM HAS NO IMPACT ON CONVENTIONAL MEMORY SIZE.  THERE IS
/	NO GENERAL EXPECTATION OF THE AVAILABILITY OF THE HIGHEST TWO PAGES OF MEMORY
/	[77400-77777] UNLESS ALL LOWER ADDRESSES IN THE ENTIRE MEMORY SPACE
/	[00000-77377] EXIST [AND ALL ARE WRITABLE].  AS SUCH, THE ROM IS ESSENTIALLY
/	"INVISIBLE" TO ALL CONFORMING COMPONENTS OF ALL OPERATING SYSTEMS COMPATIBLE
/	WITH THIS UNUSUAL MEMORY CONFIGURATION.  THE ONLY PRACTICAL CONSIDERATION IS
/	THE MAXIMUM EFFECTIVE MEMORY SIZE CAN NEVER BE THE FULL 32K ADDRESS SPACE OF
/	THE PDP-8; THE LARGEST CONVENTIONAL MEMORY SIZE POSSIBLE BECOMES 28K.

/	THE MR8E-C WAS DESIGNED FOR USE IN SMALLER CONFIGURATIONS; IF MORE MEMORY IS
/	AVAILABLE, VARIATIONS OF THE SYSTEM CONFIGURATION WILL GENERALLY BE POSSIBLE
/	TO TAKE ADVANTAGE OF ADDITIONAL MEMORY FIELDS.  THUS, ON LARGER SYSTEMS, THE
/	MAXIMUM MEMORY SIZE ACHIEVABLE WOULD BE THE USUAL 32k.  [NOTE: DURING THE
/	ECONOMIC LIFETIME OF THE TD8E AND COMPATIBLE PDP-8 MODELS, AN ADDITIONAL 4K OF
/	MEMORY MIGHT BE PROHIBITIVELY EXPENSIVE COMPARED TO THE LOWER COST OF THE
/	MR8E-C ROM; THUS, FOR MANY CUSTOMERS WITH MODEST BUDGETS, THE MR8E-C PROVED TO
/	BE A COST-EFFECTIVE ALTERNATIVE.]

/	FOR SOME CUSTOMERS, THE CONVENIENCE OF BOOTING THE SYSTEM WITHOUT HAVING TO
/	TOGGLE IN A BOOTSTRAP ROUTINE JUSTIFIED THE MI8E DIODE-BASED BOOTSTRAP LOADER,
/	A DEVICE WHICH IS DIRECTLY INITIATED FROM THE PDP-8 FRONT PANEL; THE MR8E-C
/	ROM HAS A BUILT-IN BOOTSTRAP THAT IS FUNCTIONALLY EQUIVALENT.  THUS, FOR A
/	WHOLE CLASS OF ECONOMY-MINDED CUSTOMERS, THE TD8E WITH MR8E-C CONFIGURATION
/	WAS A WISE CHOICE.
/	THERE ARE AT LEAST FOUR OPERATING SYSTEMS THAT SUPPORT THIS CONFIGURATION:

/	1)	EDU-SYSTEM 15/30 IS AN OPERATING SYSTEM BASED ON EDU-SYSTEM 30, WHICH
/		IN TURN IS BASED ON POLY BASIC; ALL OF THESE SYSTEMS ONLY SUPPORT THE
/		BASIC COMPUTER LANGUAGE.  MOST OF THE WORK ON THESE SYSTEMS WAS DONE
/		BY RICHARD LARY AND LENNY ELEKMAN WHILE STUDENTS AT THE POLYTECHNIC
/		INSTITUTE OF BROOKLYN.

/		ALL OF THESE SYSTEMS ARE DESIGNED TO RUN ON 4K MACHINES; ANY EXTENDED
/		MEMORY FIELDS [IF PRESENT] ARE IGNORED.  WHEN THE SYSTEM DEVICE IS THE
/		TD8E SIMPLE DECTAPE CONTROLLER, THE ROM ALLOWS SUPPORT OF THIS
/		SPECIFIC CONFIGURATION.  [NOTE: FIELD 7 OF READ/WRITE MEMORY IS NOT
/		ALLOWED SINCE MEMORY LOCATIONS 77400-77777 ARE ALWAYS OCCUPIED BY THE
/		MR8E-C ROM.]

/		NOTE: IN GENERAL, EDU-SYSTEM 15/30 MAY REQUIRE THE KK8F OR KK8A CPU
/		DUE TO THE USE OF INSTRUCTIONS NOT FOUND ON PDP-8 MODELS PRIOR TO THE
/		PDP-8/E; HOWEVER, IT IS STRONGLY RECOMMENDED IN THE CASE OF THE MR8E-C
/		ROM-BASED CONFIGURATION TO ONLY USE THE KK8F CPU.  THIS WOULD EXCLUDE
/		THE PDP-8/A 400 SERIES BUT WOULD ALLOW THE PDP-8/A 600 SERIES WHICH
/		USES THE KK8F CPU AS FOUND IN THE PDP-8/E, PDP-8/F AND PDP-8/M.  THE
/		SAME RECOMMENDATION APPLIES TO THE OTHER SUPPORTED OPERATING SYSTEMS.
/		THE KK8A CPU AS FOUND IN THE PDP-8/A 400 SERIES MAY NOT BE FAST ENOUGH
/		FOR RELIABLE TD8E OPERATION DUE TO THE SLOWER SPEED OF THE CPU AND THE
/		OVERHEAD ASSOCIATED WITH THE MR8E-C ROM-BASED CODE.

/	2)	OS/8 CAN BE CONFIGURED TO RUN ON ANY MODEL PDP-8 WITH AT LEAST 8K OF
/		MEMORY.  [12K IS REQUIRED TO RUN OS/8 BATCH.]  WHEN THE MR8E-C ROM IS
/		USED WITH THE TD8E, THIS DESIRABLE CONFIGURATION IS ACHIEVABLE.  AT
/		LEAST 12K OF MEMORY IS NEEDED IN OS/8 IF THE MR8E-C ROM IS NOT USED
/		[WHEN THE TD8E IS USED AS THE SYSTEM DEVICE] WHICH IMPACTS NEGATIVELY
/		ON CERTAIN OS/8 CONFIGUURATIONS.  NOTE: CERTAIN OS/8 CONFIGURATIONS
/		REQUIRE 12K BECAUSE THERE IS NO ANALOG TO THE MR8E-C AVAILABLE.  PDP-8
/		PERIPHERALS ARE SOMETIMES INADEQUATELY DESIGNED AND WILL LEAD TO
/		DECREASED OS/8 PERFORMANCE; THE TD8E WITH THE MR8E-C ALLOWS OS/8 TO
/		AVOID THIS ISSUE ON SMALLER MACHINE CONFIGURATIONS.

/	3)	COS-300/310 CAN BE CONFIGURED TO RUN ON ANY MODEL PDP-8 WITH AT LEAST
/		8K MEMORY.  [INTERNALLY, IT IS LARGELY BASED ON OS/8 AND ALL OF THE
/		CONSIDERATIONS DISCUSSED IN 2) ABOVE APPLY TO COS-300/310 AS WELL.]

/	4)	P?S/8 IS AN OPERATING SYSTEM THAT CAN RUN ON ANY MODEL PDP-8 WITH AT
/		LEAST 4K OF RAM MEMORY.  VARIOUS P?S/8 COMPONENTS AND SYSTEM PROGRAMS
/		CAN TAKE ADVANTAGE OF ALL AVAILABLE MEMORY UP TO 32K.  IN THE SPECIFIC
/		CONFIGURATION OF THE TD8E AND THE MR8E-C ROM, MEMORY SIZE IS LIMITED
/		TO AT MOST 28K DUE TO THE PRESENCE OF THE ROM IN FIELD 7.  P?S/8 CAN
/		RUN ADEQUATELY IN 4K AND ALLOWS GENERAL-PURPOSE USE IN ANY SUPPORTED
/		LANGUAGE.
/	NUMERIC LOAD DEFINITIONS.

	NL7775=	CLA CLL CMA RTL		/LOAD AC WITH 7775.
	NL7777=	CLA CMA			/LOAD AC WITH 7777.

/	MR8E-C ROM DEFINITIONS.

/	THE MR8E-C ROM IS LOCATED AT THE HIGHEST MEMORY LOCATIONS OF THE 32K ADDRESS
/	SPACE; AS SUCH, IT WILL NEVER AFFECT ANY COMPATIBLE OPERATING SYSTEM THAT
/	DYNAMICALLY DETERMINES MEMORY SIZE BY DISCOVERING COMPLETE FIELDS OF
/	READ/WRITE MEMORY.

	F2SET=	7552			/CONTINUATION OF FUNCTION CALL ADDRESS.
	GOADDR=	7340			/FIELD 0 ADDRESS FOR BOOTSTRAP STORE ROUTINE.
	ROMADR=	7400			/ROM LOADS INTO 77400-77777.
	ROMBOOT=7470			/ROM-BASED BOOTSTRAP ROUTINE ADDRESS.
	ROMFLD=	70			/ROM LOADS INTO FIELD 7.
	ROMGO=	7405			/RETRY ADDRESS AFTER ERRORS.

/	SOURCE CONTROL DEFINITIONS.

/	A SKELETAL COPY OF MEMORY LOCATIONS IN FIELD 0 RESERVED BY THE MR8E-C ROM IS
/	NORMALLY CREATED AS PART OF THIS ASSEMBLY.  [NOTE: NO ACTUAL BINARY CODE IS
/	GENERATED; THIS IS USED ONLY TO ADD CLARITY TO THE OVERALL PROGRAM LISTING.]
/	THIS ADDITIONAL LISTING SECTION CAN BE DISABLED BELOW; THIS ALLOWS INCLUSION
/	OF THE MR8E-C ROM SOURCE CODE INTO ANOTHER ASSEMBLY FOR THE PURPOSES OF
/	CREATING THE BINARY CODING OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER OF ANY OF THE
/	COMPATIBLE OPERATING SYSTEMS.

	IFNDEF	SYSKEL	<SYSKEL=1>	/GENERATE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER SKELETON.
/	SYSKEL=	0			/DO NOT GENERATE THE DEVICE HANDLER SKELETON.

/	NOTE: THE ABILITY TO CONDITIONALLY SPECIFY AN ALTERNATE FIELD FOR THE BINARY
/	OUTPUT HAS BEEN REMOVED TO ENHANCE CLARITY; THE SOURCE CODE HAS BEEN VERIFIED
/	ACCURATE BY COMPARING THE BINARY OUTPUT OBTAINED FROM AN ASSEMBLY OF THIS
/	SOURCE CODE TO A BINARY COPY OBTAINED FROM THE MR8E-C HARDWARE MODULE.

/	TD8E SIMPLE DECTAPE INPUT/OUTPUT DEFINITIONS.

	SDCODE=	77			/DEVICE CODE FOR TD8E SIMPLE DECTAPE [PRIMARY].

	SDSS=	SDCODE^10+6001		/SIMPLE DECTAPE SKIP ON SINGLE LINE FLAG.
	SDST=	SDCODE^10+6002		/SIMPLE DECTAPE SKIP IF TIME ERROR FLAG SET.
	SDSQ=	SDCODE^10+6003		/SIMPLE DECTAPE SKIP ON QUAD LINE FLAG.
	SDLC=	SDCODE^10+6004		/SIMPLE DECTAPE LOAD COMMAND REGISTER.
	SDLD=	SDCODE^10+6005		/SIMPLE DECTAPE LOAD DATA REGISTER.
	SDRC=	SDCODE^10+6006		/SIMPLE DECTAPE READ COMMAND REGISTER.
	SDRD=	SDCODE^10+6007		/SIMPLE DECTAPE READ DATA REGISTER.
/	FIELD 0 DEVICE HANDLER DEFINITIONS.

/	THE FOLLOWING LOCATIONS WITHIN THE LAST PAGE OF FIELD 0 [07600-07777] ARE
/	RESERVED BY THE MR8E-C ROM [AND IN SOME CASES ARE THE HEADERS OF COMPLETELY
/	RESERVED SUBROUTINES].  COMPATIBLE SYSTEMS MUST UTILIZE THESE LOCATIONS
/	CONSISTENTLY WITH THEIR FUNCTION WITHIN THE ROM.

/	NOTE: IF ENABLED, THE SKELETAL VERSION OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CONTAINING
/	ALL RELEVANT ROUTINES WILL ILLUSTRATE THE FULL DEPENDENCIES.  IN GENERAL, THE
/	ROM-BASED CODING REQUIRES THESE ROUTINES BE LOCATED IN FIELD 0 AS DEFINED.
/	HOWEVER, THE SPECIFIC OPERATING SYSTEM IS RESPONSIBLE FOR PLACING ALL SUCH
/	ROUTINES INTO FIELD 0 READ/WRITE MEMORY WHEN NEEDED.

/	ALL ADDRESSES NOT SHOWN IN THE SKELETAL VERSION ARE AVAILABLE TO PERFORM
/	SYSTEM-DEPENDENT FUNCTIONS ACCORDING TO THEIR SPECIFIC REQUIREMENTS.  SEVERAL
/	LOCATIONS ARE REQUIRED TO ALLOW INDIRECT ACCESS TO ROM-BASED CODING; HOWEVER,
/	THE HANDLERS CAN DESIGNATE WHERE TO PLACE THE RELEVANT POINTERS.

	BUFADR=	7751			/READ/WRITE BUFFER ADDRESS.
	BUFPTR=	7753			/BUFFER POINTER FOR READ AND STORE ROUTINES.
	CIFR=	7674			/INTERNAL RETURN ADDRESS FOR WRITING.
	DRVUNIT=7731			/DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT.
	EQUFUN=	7704			/EQUIVALENCE CHECKSUM ROUTINE.
	EQUTMP=	7750			/TEMPORARY FOR EQUIVALENCE CHECKSUM ROUTINE.
	GETWRD=	7647			/GET A WORD FROM THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
	PAGCNT=	7752			/TRANSFER PAGE COUNT.
	PUTWRD=	7654			/PUT A WORD INTO THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
	RDQUAD=	7676			/READ A 12-BIT WORD ROUTINE.
	SBLK=	7734			/LATEST DECTAPE BLOCK NUMBER.
	SCKSUM=	7733			/RUNNING CHECKSUM WORD.
	SERR=	7661			/ERROR HANDLER ADDRESS.
	SEXIT=	7722			/ROM EXIT WHEN FINISHED.
	SFUNCTI=7732			/FUNCTION WORD.
	SUNIT=	7735			/DRIVE UNIT FOR LATEST HANDLER CALL.
	TRYCNT=	7730			/RETRY COUNTER.
	WORDCT=	7754			/WORD COUNTER FOR LATEST DATA BLOCK.
	WRQ=	7724			/WRITE QUAD ROUTINE ROM CONTINUATION ADDRESS.
	WRQUAD=	7671			/WRITE A 12-BIT WORD ROUTINE.
	FIELD	ROMFLD%10		/THE ROM LOADS INTO THIS FIELD.

	*ROMADR				/LOWEST ADDRESS OF THE ROM-BASED CODE.

/	COMES HERE TO SEARCH FOR THE NEXT BLOCK IN A MULTIPLE BLOCK TRANSFER.

NXTBLK,	TAD I	PBUFADR/(BUFADRESS)	/GET THE BUFFER POINTER ADDRESS.
	TAD	ROM200/(200)		/UPDATE TO NEXT PAGE.
	DCA I   PBUFADR/(BUFADRESS)	/STORE THE BUFFER POINTER ADDRESS BACK.
	ISZ I	PSBLK/(SBLK)		/BUMP TO NEXT BLOCK.
	STL				/SECONDARY TRANSFERS ALWAYS SEARCH FORWARD.

/	COMES HERE WITH THE LINK SET TO THE COMPLEMENT OF THE DESIRED DIRECTION.

ROMGO,	CLA CML RTR			/INVERT LINK AND MOVE TO DIRECTION BIT.
        TAD     ROM1200/(1200)		/ADD ON GO BIT [ONLY HIGHEST FOUR BITS USED].
        TAD I   PSUNIT/(SUNIT)		/ADD ON DRIVE UNIT BIT.
        SDLC				/LOAD THE COMMAND REGISTER.

/	WE NEED TO DELAY TWO TAPE WORDS BEFORE CHECKING THE MARK REGISTER.

	SDSQ				/FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, CLEAR THE FLAG.
	SDSQ				/FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, CLEAR THE FLAG.

/	CHECK FOR END ZONE OR BLOCK MARK VALUES.

SRCHMOR,SDSS				/SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	CLL RTL				/MOVE THE DIRECTION BIT TO THE LINK; THE
					/MARK WINDOW IS MOVED TO AC BITS[4-9].
	AND	ROM374/(374)		/ISOLATE MARK TRACK BITS.
	TAD	ROM7670/(-110)		/COMPARE TO END ZONE MARK VALUE.
	SNA				/SKIP IF OTHER.
	JMP	ENDZONE			/HANDLE END ZONE THERE.
	TAD	ROM7760/(-130+110)	/COMPARE TO BLOCK MARK VALUE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	SRCHMORE		/KEEP GOING IF NEITHER MATCH.
/	A BLOCK MARK WAS DETECTED WHILE SEARCHING FOR THE DESIRED BLOCK.  THE LATEST
/	BLOCK VALUE AND DIRECTION OF TAPE MOTION WILL BE TESTED ACCORDINGLY.

/	DECTAPE SEARCHING RULES.

/	1)	WHILE SEARCHING FORWARDS, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS LOWER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK, CONTINUE THE SEARCH MOVING FORWARDS.

/	2)	WHILE SEARCHING FORWARDS, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS THE DESIRED BLOCK,
/		THEN SWITCH TO READ OR WRITE AS REQUIRED.

/	3)	WHILE SEARCHING FORWARDS, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS HIGHER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK, THE TAPE MUST BE REVERSED.  [NOTE: IF THE FORWARD END
/		ZONE IS DETECTED, THE TAPE MOTION MUST BE REVERSED.]

/	4)	WHILE SEARCHING IN REVERSE, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS HIGHER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR, CONTINUE THE SEARCH
/		MOVING IN REVERSE.

/	5)	WHILE SEARCHING IN REVERSE, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS AT THE BLOCK EQUAL
/		TO THE DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR, THE TAPE SHOULD BE
/		TURNED AROUND.  ONCE THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND UP TO SPEED, 2)
/		ABOVE WILL APPLY.  [NOTE: FOR VERY LOW BLOCK NUMBERS, THIS WILL NEVER
/		HAPPEN; HOWEVER, THE END ZONE WILL BE DETECTED AND THE TAPE MOTION
/		MUST BE REVERSED.  THERE IS SUFFICIENT INTER-BLOCK ZONE WORDS JUST
/		AFTER THE END ZONE TO ALLOW DETECTION OF BLOCK 0000 AFTER TURNING THE
/		TAPE AROUND IN THE END ZONE.]

/	6)	WHILE SEARCHING IN REVERSE, IF THE LATEST BLOCK NUMBER IS LOWER THAN
/		THE DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR, THE TAPE MOTION MUST BE
/		REVERSED.  ONCE THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND UP TO SPEED, 1) ABOVE
/		APPLIES UNLESS NOW AT THE DESIRED BLOCK, IN WHICH CASE 2) APPLIES.

/	NOTE: IN MOST DECTAPE SEARCH IMPLEMENTATIONS, THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR IS USUALLY
/	0002; HOWEVER, THE MORE CONSERVATIVE VALUE OF 0003 IS USED IN MOST TD8E
/	APPLICATIONS.  IT IS THEORIZED THAT THE EARLIEST TD8E SEARCH ROUTINE WAS
/	COPIED FROM A VERY OLD IMPLEMENTATION OF A DEVICE HANDLER FOR TC01/TC08
/	DECTAPE THAT WAS OBSOLETE; MOST TC01/TC08 SEARCH ROUTINES USE AN UNDERSHOOT
/	FACTOR OF 0002.  THERE ARE NO REPORTED PROBLEMS IN ANY SITUATION WHERE
/	PROPERLY ADJUSTED TU55 OR TU56 DRIVES ARE USED.  ALL KNOWN TD8E ROUTINES
/	WRITTEN BY DEC PERSONNEL CLEARLY IMITATE THE ORIGINAL TD8E SUBROUTINE THAT WAS
/	RELEASED PRIOR TO THE MR8E-C ROM.  AS SUCH, TURN-AROUND PERFORMANCE ON ALL
/	TD8E SYSTEMS RUNNING DEC SOFTWARE IS SOMEWHAT SLUGGISH.

/	SEARCH ALGORITHM DETAILS.

/	THE LATEST BLOCK IS COMPARED TO THE DESIRED BLOCK USING ONE'S COMPLEMENT
/	ARITHMETIC; THE LINK IS CLEAR IF THE CURRENT TAPE MOTION IS FORWARD AND SET IF
/	THE CURRENT TAPE MOTION IS IN THE REVERSE DIRECTION.
/	POSSIBLE SCENARIOS:

/	1)	THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND POSITIONED AT A BLOCK LOWER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK.  THE LINK IS CLEAR INDICATING FORWARD MOTION.  THE
/		COMPARISON FAILS, AND THE LINK IS SET.  AS SUCH, THE SEARCH CONTINUES
/		WITHOUT CHANGING THE DIRECTION OF MOTION.

/	2)	THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND POSITIONED AT THE DESIRED BLOCK.  THE
/		LINK IS CLEAR INDICATING FORWARD MOTION.  THE COMPARISON SUCCEEDS AND
/		THE LINK IS SET.  AS SUCH, THE TAPE CAN BE READ TO LOCATE ADDITIONAL
/		DATA WORDS TO POSITION THE TAPE FOR EITHER READING OR WRITING.

/	3)	THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND POSITIONED PAST THE DESIRED BLOCK.
/		THE LINK IS CLEAR INDICATING FORWARD MOTION.  THE COMPARISON FAILS AND
/		THE LINK STAYS CLEAR.  AS SUCH, THE TAPE MOTION NEEDS TO BE REVERSED.

/	4)	THE TAPE IS MOVING IN REVERSE AND POSITIONED AT A BLOCK HIGHER THAN
/		THE DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR.  THE LINK IS SET
/		INDICATING REVERSE MOTION.  THE COMPARISON FAILS AND THE LINK STAYS
/		SET.  AS SUCH, THE SEARCH CONTINUES WITHOUT CHANGING THE DIRECTION OF
/		MOTION.

/	5)	THE TAPE IS MOVING IN REVERSE AND POSITIONED AT THE DESIRED BLOCK
/		MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR.  THE LINK IS SET INDICATING REVERSE
/		MOTION.  THE COMPARISON SUCCEEDS AND THE LINK IS CLEARED.  AS SUCH,
/		THE TAPE MOTION NEEDS TO BE REVERSED.

/	6)	THE TAPE IS MOVING IN REVERSE AND POSITIONED AT A BLOCK LOWER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR.  THE LINK IS SET INDICATING
/		REVERSE MOTION.  THE COMPARISON FAILS AND THE LINK IS CLEARED.  AS
/		SUCH, THE TAPE MOTION NEEDS TO BE REVERSED.

/	IN ALL CASES, DETECTION OF THE END ZONE REQUIRES REVERSAL OF TAPE DIRECTION.

	SDRD				/GET THE LATEST BLOCK NUMBER
	SZL				/SKIP IF GOING FORWARDS.
	TAD	ROM3/(3)		/ADD ON UNDERSHOOT FACTOR IF MOVING BACKWARDS.
	CMA				/INVERT FOR ONE'S COMPLEMENT COMPARISON.
	TAD I	PSBLK/(SBLK)		/COMPARE TO DESIRED BLOCK.
	CMA				/INVERT BACK FOR TEST.
	SNA				/SKIP IF NOT THE DESIRED BLOCK.
	JMP	BLKFND			/JUMP IF CORRECT BLOCK TO TEST FURTHER.
ROM7670,SNA SZL CLA			/SKIP IF THE LINK IS CLEAR; THE SNA IS
					/EXTRANEOUS AND USED TO FORM THE CONSTANT 7670.
	JMP	SRCHMORE		/KEEP GOING IN THE SAME DIRECTION IF LINK SET.

/	THE TAPE IS MOVING IN A DIRECTION COUNTER TO THE CURRENT INTENDED DIRECTION.
/	IF THE TAPE IS MOVING IN REVERSE OR THE REVERSE END ZONE HAS BEEN DETECTED,
/	THE DIRECTION OF MOTION MUST BE REVERSED.  THE LINK IS CLEAR WHICH WILL CAUSE
/	THE TAPE TO BE TURNED AROUND.

/	IF THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARD OR THE FORWARD END ZONE HAS BEEN DETECTED, AN
/	ERROR CONDITION IS INDICATED [WHICH CAN BE TOLERATED UP TO THREE TIMES].
ENDZONE,SDRC				/READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	CLL RTL				/DIRECTION BIT TO LINK.
	SZL CLA				/SKIP IF TOO FAR UP THE TAPE OR IN THE END ZONE.
	JMP	ROMGO			/GO TURN THE TAPE AROUND THERE AND CONTINUE THE
					/SEARCH OPERATION.

/	AT THIS POINT, MOVING FORWARD IS CONSIDERED AN ERROR.

	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMP I	PSERR/(SERR)		/CHECK THE RETRY COUNT AND CONTINUE THERE.

/	WE ARE POSITIONED AT THE PROPER BLOCK NUMBER; CHECK IF THE TAPE IS MOVING
/	FORWARD; IF MOVING IN THE REVERSE DIRECTION, THE LINK IS CLEAR WHICH WILL
/	ALLOW THE TAPE TO TURN AROUND.

BLKFND,	SZL CLA				/SKIP IF MOVING FORWARD AT THE DESIRED BLOCK.
	JMP	ROMGO			/GO THERE TO TURN THE TAPE AROUND.

/	THE TAPE IS PROPERLY POSITIONED; CONTINUE SETTING UP THE DATA TRANSFER.

	TAD I	PBUFADR/(BUFADRESS)	/GET THE BUFFER ADDRESS FOR THE DATA TRANSFER.
	JMP I	PRWCONT/(RWCONT)	/CONTINUE THERE.	

ROM7600,7600				/CONSTANT 7600.
ROM200,	200				/CONSTANT 0200.
ROM374,	374				/CONSTANT 0374.
ROM7760,7760				/CONSTANT 7760.
ROM3,	3				/CONSTANT 0003.
PBUFADR,BUFADR				/POINTER TO BUFFER ADDRESS.
PSBLK,	SBLK				/POINTER TO LATEST BLOCK TO TRANSFER.
PSUNIT,	SUNIT				/POINTER TO SUNIT.
PSERR,	SERR				/POINTER TO SERR.
PRWCONT,RWCONT				/POINTER TO THE REST OF THE READ/WRITE CODE.
/	BUILT-IN BOOTSTRAP STARTS HERE.  SET THE FRONT-PANEL SWITCHES TO 7470.  PRESS
/	EXTENDED ADDRESS LOAD, ADDRESS LOAD, CLEAR AND THEN CONTINUE TO GET HERE.

ROMBOOT,CDF	00			/ENSURE FIELD 0 FOR INDIRECT STORAGE.

/	A SUBROUTINE MUST BE COPIED INTO FIELD 0 TO READ THE DATA WORDS ON THE DECTAPE
/	INTO MEMORY ACCORDING TO THE STANDARD TD8E CONVENTIONS.

        TAD     STRWRD+1+RLFACTOR	/GET "DCA I STRPTR".
	DCA I	PSTRW1/(STRWRD+1)	/STORE IT IN FIELD 0 WHERE IT EXECUTES.
        TAD     STRWRD+2+RLFACTOR	/GET "CIF 70".
	DCA I	PSTRW2/(STRWRD+2)	/STORE IT IN FIELD 0 WHERE IT EXECUTES.
        TAD     STRWRD+3+RLFACTOR	/GET "JMP I STRWRD".
	DCA I	PSTRW3/(STRWRD+3)	/STORE IT IN FIELD 0 WHERE IT EXECUTES.
        TAD     STRPTR+RLFACTOR		/GET "BOOTBUFFER".
	DCA I	PSTRPTR/(STRPTR)	/STORE IT IN FIELD 0 WHERE IT IS USED.

/	REWIND THE TAPE UNTIL REVERSE END ZONE MARKS ARE FOUND.

	TAD	RM3000/(3000)		/GET REVERSE DIRECTION AND GO BITS [ON UNIT 0].
	SDLC				/LOAD THE COMMAND REGISTER TO START REWINDING.
EZLOOP,	SDSS				/SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ IN THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	RM77/(77)		/JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.
	TAD	ROM7756/(-22)		/COMPARE TO END ZONE MARK VALUE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	EZLOOP			/KEEP GOING IF NOT.

/	THE END ZONE HAS BEEN FOUND WHILE GOING BACKWARDS.  WE NEED TO TURN THE
/	DECTAPE AROUND AND SEARCH FORWARD FOR THE 31 CODE [PART OF THE BLOCK MARK AND
/	THE REVERSE GUARD MARK].

ROM1200,TAD	ROM1200/(1200)		/SET UNIT 0 AND GO BITS TO MOVE FORWARD; [NOTE:
					/ONLY BITS[0-3] ARE USED.]
	SDLC				/START THE DECTAPE MOVING FORWARD.
LOOP31,	SDSS				/SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ IN THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	RM77/(77)		/JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.
	TAD	ROM7747/(-31)		/COMPARE TO 31 CODE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES
	JMP	LOOP31			/GO BACK IF NOT.
/	WE ARE POSITIONED AT THE 31 MARK WINDOW VALUE.  START STORING DATA WORDS WHERE
/	THEY BELONG.  [NOTE: THE FIRST FOUR ARE BEFORE THE DATA WORDS OF BLOCK 0000.]

BTRDLP,	SDSQ				/QUAD FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, READ IN A DATA WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS I	PSTRWRD/(STRWRD)	/STORE A DATA WORD.
	ISZ I	PSTRPTR/(STRPTR)	/BUMP TO NEXT; MORE TO DO?
	JMP	BTRDLP			/YES, KEEP GOING.
	CIF	00			/NO, GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMP	STARTIT			/STARTUP THE BOOT CODE FROM THE DECTAPE.

ROM7756,7756				/CONSTANT 7756.
ROM7747,7747				/CONSTANT 7747.
RM3000,	3000				/CONSTANT 3000.
RM77,	77				/CONSTANT 0077.
PSTRWRD,STRWRD				/POINTER TO "STRWRD" ROUTINE IN FIELD 0.
PSTRW1,	STRWRD+1			/POINTER TO "STRWRD+1" IN FIELD 0.
PSTRW2,	STRWRD+2			/POINTER TO "STRWRD+2" IN FIELD 0.
PSTRW3,	STRWRD+3			/POINTER TO "STRWRD+3" IN FIELD 0.
PSTRPTR,STRPTR				/POINTER TO "STRPTR" IN FIELD 0.
	RMADDR=	.			/WHERE THE FOLLOWING CODE IS LOCATED WITHIN
					/THE ROM.

	NOPUNCH				/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	FIELD	0			/WHERE THIS LOADS.

	ENPUNCH				/UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	RELOC	GOADDR			/WHERE THE FOLLOWING CODE WILL BE
					/LOADED IN FIELD 0 WHEN EXECUTED.

	RLFACTO=RMADDR-.		/RELOCATION FACTOR FOR MOVED WORDS.

/	SUBROUTINE TO STORE DATA WORDS READ IN FROM THE DECTAPE INTO MEMORY; ALL WORDS
/	WILL BE LOCATED HERE WHEN EXECUTED.

STRWRD,	.-.				/STORE A WORD ROUTINE.
	DCA I	STRPTR			/STORE AC INTO MEMORY
	CIF	70			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	STRWRD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

STRPTR,	BOOTBUFFER			/WHERE THE TAPE DATA IS STORED.

	GOEND=	.			/END OF RAM-LOADED CODE.

	RELOC				/TURN OFF RELOCATION.

	FIELD	ROMFLD%10		/RESTORE ROM-LOADING FIELD.

	*GOEND-GOADDR+RMADDR		/RESTORE ROM ADDRESS.
/	COMES HERE TO CONTINUE THE CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.

/	SEVERAL PARAMETERS ARE SETUP BY THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER:

/	1)	THE DRIVE UNIT FOR THE TRANSFER IS PLACED IN BIT[0] OF "SUNIT".

/	2)	THE FIELD FOR DATA TRANSFER FROM THE CALLER'S DATA BUFFER IS PLACED IN
/		"GETFLD" IN THE FORM OF "CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE FIELD OF THE CALLER'S
/		DATA BUFFER.  THIS INSTRUCTION IS CONTAINED IN THE "GETWRD" ROUTINE
/		LATER CALLED FROM ROM-BASED ROUTINES FOR WRITING TO THE DECTAPE.

/	3)	THE FIELD FOR DATA TRANSFERS TO THE CALLER'S DATA BUFFER IS PLACED IN
/		"PUTFLD" IN THE FORM OF "CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE FIELD OF THE CALLER'S
/		DATA BUFFER.  THIS INSTRUCTION IS CONTAINED IN THE "PUTWRD" ROUTINE
/		LATER CALLED FROM ROM-BASED ROUTINES FOR READING FROM THE DECTAPE.

/	4)	"BUFPTR" IS SET TO THE LOWEST ADDRESS OF THE CALLER'S DATA BUFFER.

/	5)	THE STARTING BLOCK FOR THE LATEST TRANSFER IS PLACED INTO "SBLK".  THE
/		VALUE WILL BE INCREMENTED FOR EACH BLOCK TRANSFERRED.  [AS SUCH, IT IS
/		POSSIBLE TO DERIVE THE PROPER INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION FROM THE
/		PREVIOUS VALUE BY COMPARING TO THE LATEST CALLER'S BLOCK VALUE.]

/	6)	THE DATA REGISTER IS SET TO THE FUNCTION WORD AS DEFINED IN OS/8,
/		COS-300/310 AND P?S/8 [WITH AN EXCEPTION NOTED BELOW]:

/		A) THE READ/WRITE FUNCTION IS PASSED IN BIT[0]; 1=WRITE, 0=READ.

/		B) THE PAGE COUNT FOR THE CALL IS CONTAINED IN BITS[1-5]; IF THIS
/		   VALUE IS SET TO 00000 THEN TRANSFER AN ENTIRE FIELD [32 PAGES].

/		C) BITS[6-10] ARE IGNORED.

/		D) BIT[11] IS SET TO THE INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION FOR THE TRANSFER.
/		   NOTE: OS/8 ONLY SETS THIS BIT IN THE FEW INSTANCES WITHIN OS/8
/		   WHERE THIS INFORMATION IS KNOWN ON A STATIC BASIS.  OPERATING
/		   SYSTEMS SUCH AS P?S/8 MAY CALCULATE THIS VALUE DYNAMICALLY FOR ALL
/		   DEVICE HANDLER CALLS TO PROVIDE NOTICABLY SUPERIOR PERFORMANCE.
/		   WITHIN EACH OPERATING SYSTEM THERE IS NO UNIVERSALLY AGREED UPON
/		   DEFINITION FOR THIS BIT IN TERMS OF THE CALLER'S FUNCTION WORD.
/		   THE INITIAL GUESS AS DEFINED ABOVE ONLY APPLIES TO OS/8 AND
/		   COS-300/310.  WITHIN P?S/8 THIS BIT IS DEFINED AS THE LOGICAL DRIVE
/		   UNIT [0 OR 1], THUS THE NORMAL MEANING OF THE BIT MUST BE MASKED.
/		   IF THERE IS SUFFICIENT ADDITIONAL CODE SPACE IN THE HANDLER [WHICH
/		   MAY BE ENHANCED BY OTHER OPTIONAL SYSTEM COMPONENTS OF THE LOGICAL
/		   CONSOLE OVERLAY], THE DYNAMIC INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION WILL BE
/		   CALCULATED AND INCLUDED IN THE DATA REGISTER.  IN EDU-SYSTEM 15/30
/		   THIS BIT IS ALWAYS CLEAR; AS SUCH, ALL CALLS START THE DECTAPE IN
/		   THE REVERSE DIRECTION.

/		NOTE: WHILE OS/8 SPARSELY USES THE POTENTIALLY FORWARD INITIAL SEARCH
/		DIRECTION, THE DYNAMIC METHOD USED IN P?S/8 MAY BE SUCCESSFUL UP TO AS
/		MUCH AS 40% OF THE TIME DEPENDING ON SPECIFIC SYSTEM USAGE [SUCH AS
/		CAREFUL PLACEMENT OF FILES TO TAKE ADVANTAGE OF FORWARD INITIAL SEARCH
/		SITUATIONS].
F2SET,	TAD I	PSUNIT/(SUNIT)		/GET THE DRIVE UNIT WITHOUT THE GO BIT LOADED
	SDLC				/INTO THE COMMAND REGISTER [TAPE IS STOPPED].
	NL7775				/SETUP THE
	DCA I	PTRYCNT/(TRYCNT)	/RETRY COUNTER.
	SDRC				/GET THE UNIT BIT.
	DCA I	PDRVUNIT/(DRVUNIT)	/STASH IT THERE.
	SDRD				/GET FUNCTION WORD BACK FROM THE DATA REGISTER.
	CLL RAL				/MOVE UP AND
	AND	ROM7600/(7600)		/ISOLATE PAGE BITS.
	DCA I	PPAGCNT/(PAGCNT)	/SAVE AS TRANSFER PAGE COUNTER.
	SDRD				/GET FUNCTION WORD BACK FROM THE DATA REGISTER.
	DCA I	PSFUNCT/(SFUNCTION)	/STASH THE FUNCTION WORD.
	SDRD				/GET FUNCTION WORD BACK FROM THE DATA REGISTER.
	RAR				/INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION TO LINK.
	JMP	ROMGO			/CONTINUE HERE

PTRYCNT,TRYCNT				/POINTER TO WHERE RETRY COUNT IS STORED.
PDRVUNI,DRVUNIT				/POINTER TO WHERE DRIVE UNIT IS STORED.
PPAGCNT,PAGCNT				/POINTER TO WHERE TRANSFER PAGE COUNT IS STORED.
PSFUNCT,SFUNCTION			/POINTER TO WHERE FUNCTION WORD IS STORED.

	ZBLOCK	.&7600+200-.		/EMPTY SPACE.
	PAGE				/NEXT PAGE.

/	COMES HERE TO CONTINUE THE LATEST READ/WRITE OPERATION.

/	THE AC CONTAINS THE LATEST BUFFER ADDRESS.

RWCONT,	DCA I	PBUFPTR/(BUFPTR)	/SAVE THE BUFFER ADDRESS.
        TAD     RM7600/(-200)		/SETUP THE TRANSFER WORD
        DCA I   PWORDCT/(WORDCT)	/COUNT FOR LATEST BLOCK.

/	NOW LOCATE THE REVERSE GUARD MARK.

RGLOOP,	SDSS				/FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	ROM77/(77)		/JUST THE MARK WINDOW BITS.
	TAD	ROMM32/(-32)		/COMPARE TO REVERSE GUARD MARK VALUE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	RGLOOP			/ELSE GO WAIT FOR IT.
	TAD I	PSFUN2/(SFUNCTION)	/GET THE FUNCTION WORD.
ROM7700,SMA CLA				/SKIP IF WRITING; ALSO USED AS CONSTANT 7700.
	JMP	READIT			/JUMP IF READING.
	SDRC				/GET THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	ROM300/(300)		/JUST WRITE-LOCKOUT AND SELECT/TIME ERROR BITS.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0 EITHER WAY.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF NO ERRORS.
	JMP	SERR			/GO THERE TO COMPLAIN ABOUT ERRORS.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/READ THE REVERSE GUARD WORD.
RM7600,	CLA!400				/CLEAN UP; ALSO USED AS CONSTANT 7600.
	TAD	ROM1400/(1400)		/GET GO AND WRITE BITS.
        TAD I   PDRVU2/(DRVUNIT)	/ADD ON DRIVE UNIT BIT.
        SDLC				/START UP THE WRITE OPERATION.
	NL7777				/SET ALL ONES.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	WRQUAD			/WRITE REVERSE PCC [CHECKSUM] WORD.
	NL7777				/SETUP ALL ONES AGAIN.
	DCA I	PSCKSUM/(SCKSUM)	/SETUP THE INITIAL CHECKSUM.
WRILUP,	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	GETWRD			/GET A DATA WORD FROM THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	CIF CDF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
        JMS     WRQUAD			/WRITE OUT A DATA WORD.
        ISZ I   PBUFPTR/(BUFPTR)	/BUMP TO NEXT.
ROM77,	77				/CONSTANT 0077; PLACED HERE IN CASE IT SKIPS.
        ISZ I   PWORDCT/(WORDCT)	/DONE ENOUGH DATA WORDS?
        JMP     WRILUP			/NO, GO BACK FOR MORE.
	CIF	00			/GOTO FIELD 0.
	JMS	WRQUAD			/WRITE 0000 AS 129TH DATA WORD.
        JMP     RWCOMMON		/CONTINUE THERE TO WRITE OUT THE CHECKSUM.
/	COMES HERE WITH CHECKSUM IN AC[0-5] IF WRITING.

WRITEND,CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	WRQUAD			/WRITE THE PCC WORD.

/	AN EXTRA WRITE IS USED TO ENSURE THE CHECKSUM IS WRITTEN.  [APPARENTLY, THE
/	TD8E WRITE OPERATION IS INHERENTLY DOUBLE-BUFFERED, THUS EACH WRITE OPERATION
/	MERELY SETS UP THE DATA FOR THE NEXT WORD.]

	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	WRQUAD			/THIS FINISHES THE CHECKSUM WRITING.

/	FALLS THROUGH IF WRITING WITH A CLEAR AC.  IF READING, THE CHECKSUM IS IN THE
/	AC WHICH [HOPEFULLY] IS ZERO.

READEND,CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0 EITHER WAY.
	SDST				/TIMING ERROR?
	SZA CLA				/NOT A TIMING ERROR, IS THE CHECKSUM CLEAR?
	JMP	SERR			/JUMP IF EITHER ERROR.	
	TAD I	PPAGC2/(PAGCNT)		/GET THE CURRENT PAGE COUNTER.
        TAD     RM7600/(-200)		/SUBTRACT ONE PAGE.
	SNA				/SKIP IF STILL MORE TO GO.
	JMP	SEXIT			/ALL ROM OPERATIONS ARE DONE; RETURN TO HANDLER.
	DCA I	PPAGC2/(PAGCNT)		/STORE BACK UPDATED PAGE COUNT.
	CIF	70			/STAYING IN FIELD 7 AFTER ALL.
	JMP I	PNXTBLK/(NXTBLK)	/GO DO ANOTHER BLOCK.

/	COMES HERE IF READING.

READIT,	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/READ THE REVERSE GUARD WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/READ THE LOCK WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/GET THE REVERSE PCC WORD.
	AND	ROM77/(77)		/JUST THE CHECKSUM BITS.
	TAD	ROM7700/(7700)		/ADD ON SET HIGH-ORDER BITS.
	DCA I	PSCKSUM/(SCKSUM)	/STORE INITIAL CHECKSUM VALUE.
READLP,	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/GET A DATA WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/INCLUDE DATA IN RUNNING CHECKSUM.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	PUTWRD			/STORE THE DATA WORD IN THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	CDF	00			/BACK TO FIELD 0.
	ISZ I	PBUFPTR/(BUFPTR)	/BUMP TO NEXT ADDRESS.
ROM300,	300				/CONSTANT 0300; PLACED HERE IN CASE IT SKIPS.
	ISZ I	PWORDCT/(WORDCT)	/DONE ALL DATA WORDS?
	JMP	READLP			/NO, KEEP GOING.
/	GET THE 129TH [FINAL] WORD [CHECKSUMMED ONLY, NOT TRANSFERRED].

	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/GET THE FINAL DATA WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/INCLUDE FINAL DATA WORD IN RUNNING CHECKSUM.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/GET THE PCC WORD.
	AND	ROM7700/(7700)		/JUST CHECKSUM BITS.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/INCLUDE IN RUNNING CHECKSUM.
	JMP	RWCOMMON		/CONTINUE THERE.

/	THIS CODE CONTINUES THE WRQUAD SUBROUTINE IN FIELD 0.

WRQ,	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/LATEST DATA WORD .EQU. CHECKSUM.
	SDSQ				/QUAD FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDLD				/YES, SEND LATEST DATA WORD.
	CLA CLL				/CLEAN UP
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMP	CIFR			/CONTINUE THERE TO RETURN TO ORIGINAL CALLER.

/	COMES HERE AT THE END OF THE LATEST BLOCK READ/WRITE OPERATION AFTER THE DATA
/	IS PROCESSED.  [NOTE: THE DATA FIELD IS 00.]

RWCOMMO,CLA				/CLEAN UP.
	TAD I	PSCKSUM/(SCHKSUM)	/GET THE ACCUMULATED CHECKSUM.
	CLL CMA RTL;RTL;RTL		/INVERT AND MOVE UP SIX BITS.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/EQUIVALENCE LOW-ORDER HALF TO HIGH-ORDER HALF.
	CLA CLL				/CLEAN UP.
	TAD I	PSFUN2/(SFUNCTION)	/GET THE FUNCTION WORD.
	RAL				/MOVE READ/WRITE BIT TO LINK
	CLA				/THROW THE REST AWAY.
	TAD I	PSCKSUM/(SCHKSUM)	/GET THE FINAL CHECKSUM VALUE.
	AND	ROM7700/(7700)		/JUST THE SIX-BIT CHECKSUM.
	SNL				/SKIP IF WRITING.
	JMP	READEND			/JUMP IF READING.

/	WE NEED TO WRITE OUT THE PCC MARK (CHECKSUM) WORD.

	JMP	WRITEND			/CONTINUE THERE.
PBUFPTR,BUFPTR				/POINTER TO DATA BUFFER POINTER.
PWORDCT,WORDCT				/POINTER TO WORD COUNTER.
ROMM32,	-32				/CONSTANT 7746.
PSFUN2,	SFUNCTION			/POINTER TO FUNCTION WORD.
ROM1400,1400				/CONSTANT 1400.
PDRVU2,	DRVUNIT				/POINTER TO DRIVE UNIT.
PSCKSUM,SCKSUM				/POINTER TO CHECKSUM WORD.
PPAGC2,	PAGCNT				/POINTER TO PAGE COUNT.
PNXTBLK,NXTBLK				/POINTER TO NEXT BLOCK ROUTINE.

	ZBLOCK	.&7600+200-.		/EMPTY SPACE.

	ROMEND=	.			/END OF ROM CODE.
	NOPUNCH				/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	FIELD	0			/LOADS IN FIELD 0.

/	THE ROM-BASED BUILT-IN BOOTSTRAP IS COMPATIBLE WITH THE STANDALONE TD8E
/	BOOTSTRAP; SOME OF THE LOADING CONVENTIONS ARE SHARED BY BOTH ROUTINES.

/	THE STANDALONE BOOTSTRAP FOLLOWS TO DEFINE THE RELEVANT LOADING AND STARTING
/	ROUTINES.

	*7300				/THIS IS WHERE THE STANDALONE BOOTSTRAP LOADS.

K1000,	TAD	GET/(2000)		/GET REVERSE DIRECTION BIT.  ALSO CONSTANT
					/1000 [ONLY THE HIGH-ORDER FOUR BITS ARE USED].
	JMS	GET			/LOCATE REVERSE END ZONE.
	JMS	GET			/LOOK FOR 31 CODE GOING FORWARD.
RD,	SDSQ				/QUAD FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, READ IT IN.
	DCA I	WCT			/STORE IT INTO MEMORY.
	ISZ	WCT			/BUMP TO NEXT; ALL DONE YET?
	JMP	RD			/NO, KEEP GOING.

/	AT THIS POINT BLOCK 0000 HAS BEEN READ INTO 07360-07557 AND BLOCK 0001 HAS
/	BEEN READ INTO 07600-07777.  [NOTE: ONLY OS/8 AND COS-300/310 REQUIRE THE
/	BLOCK OOO1 DATA LOAD; EDU-SYSTEM 15/30 AND P?S/8 REQUIRE ONLY BLOCK 0000 BE
/	LOADED INTO MEMORY; THE OFFSET WILL BE EXPECTED AND DEALT WITH ACCORDINGLY.]

	JMP I	STRT/(STARTIT)		/GO START IT UP.

/	GET TO DESIRED MARK WINDOW VALUE ROUTINE.  [NOTE: INITIALIZED TO SAVE A WORD.]

GET,	2000				/GET TO DESIRED MARK ROUTINE.
	TAD	K1000/(1000)		/ADD ON UNIT [0] AND GO BITS TO DIRECTION BIT
					/PASSED IN AC.  NOTE: LOW-ORDER BITS IGNORED.
	SDLC				/LOAD COMMAND REGISTER TO START THE TAPE MOVING.
MRKSRCH,SDSS				/NEW LINE IN?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	K77/(77)		/JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.

/	THE FIRST TIME THIS ROUTINE IS USED, THE REVERSE END ZONE MARK VALUE IS BEING
/	SEARCHED FOR; THE SECOND TIME, THE 31 CODE IS BEING SEARCHED FOR.

MARKSEL,TAD	MARK			/COMPARE TO DESIRED [END MARK OR 31 CODE].
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	MRKSRCH			/GO BACK IF NOT.
	ISZ	MARKSEL			/BUMP TO NEXT MARK CODE FOR NEXT TIME.
	JMP I	GET			/RETURN TO CALLER.

WCT,	BOOTBUFFER			/WORD COUNT AND STORE POINTER.
MARK,	-22				/REVERSE END ZONE MARK CONSTANT.
	-31				/31 CODE MARK CONSTANT.
K77,	77				/CONSTANT 0077.
STRT,	STARTIT				/WHERE TO START THE BOOT CODE READ IN.
	*7354				/TAPE BLOCK 0000 CONTENTS STARTS LOADING HERE.

BOOTBUF,ZBLOCK	4			/THESE WORDS ARE LOADED BEFORE THE DATA WORDS.

	BLKDATA=.			/THE DATA WORDS IN BLOCK 0000 ARE LOADED HERE.

	ZBLOCK	7400-.			/THE FIRST 0020 WORDS OF BLOCK 0000 LOAD HERE.

	STARTIT=.			/THE REST OF THE WORDS OF BLOCK 0000 LOAD HERE
					/AND CONTROL IS TRANSFERRED HERE; THE CODE
					/LOADED HERE NEEDS TO BE AWARE OF THIS TO
					/CONTINUE THE SYSTEM-SPECIFIC BOOTUP PROCESS.
	XLIST	OFF			/TURN LISTING OFF.

	IFNZRO	SYSKEL	<

	XLIST	ON
	FIELD	0			/WHERE SUPPORT ROUTINES MUST BE LOADED.

	PAGE	37			/IN VARIOUS LOCATIONS OF THE LAST PAGE.

/	CERTAIN ROUTINES WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER MUST BE LOCATED AT SPECIFIC
/	ADDRESSES AS DETERMINED WITHIN THE ROM.  ALL MEMORY LOCATIONS DEFINED BELOW IN
/	FIELD 0 MUST BE LOADED ACCORDINGLY; IT IS THE RESPONSIBILITY OF THE OPERATING
/	SYSTEM TO LOAD THE CORRECT VALUES INTO THE STATED MEMORY LOCATIONS.

/	THE OPERATING SYSTEM SETS UP THE ROM-DESIGNATED VARIABLES FROM THE PARAMETERS
/	PASSED TO IT ACCORDING TO THE SYSTEM'S SUBROUTINE CALL CONVENTIONS.  THIS
/	INCLUDES PASSING SOME ASPECT OF THE FUNCTION WORD OR OTHER DATA IN THE TD8E
/	DATA REGISTER [USING THE "SDLD" INSTRUCTION].  WHEN ALL VARIABLES ARE SET, THE
/	ROM IS ENTERED BY THE FOLLOWING:

/	CDF	00			/SETUP OUR FIELD
/	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
/	JMP I	PF2SET/(F2SET)		/START UP THE ROM TO INITIATE THE CALL.

/	SUPPORT ROUTINES FOR THE ROM-BASED CODE.

	*GETWRD				/WHERE THIS BELONGS.

GETWRD,	.-.				/GET A WORD FROM THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
GETFLD,	.-.^10+CDF			/WILL BE "CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE
					/DATA FIELD OF THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	TAD I	BUFPTR			/GET A WORD FROM THE BUFFER.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	GETWRD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

PUTWRD,	.-.				/PUT A WORD INTO THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
PUTFLD,	.-.^10+CDF			/WILL BE "CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE
					/DATA FIELD OF THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	DCA I	BUFPTR			/PUT A WORD INTO THE BUFFER.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	PUTWRD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

/	ERRORS COME HERE.  [NOTE: NOT ALL OPERATING SYSTEMS HANDLE ERRORS IN THE SAME
/	EXACT MANNER; WHAT FOLLOWS IS TYPICAL FOR SOME SUPPORTED SYSTEMS.]

	SERR=	.			/ERROR HANDLING ROUTINE HERE.

/	CLA CLL				/CLEAN UP.
/	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
/	ISZ	TRYCNT			/TOO MANY ERRORS?
/	JMP I	PROMGO/(ROMGO)		/NO, TRY AGAIN.

/	TOO MANY RETRIES; HANDLE FATAL ERROR HERE.

/	TAD	SUNIT			/GET THE CURRENT TAPE UNIT.
/	SDLC				/STOP THE TAPE.
	*WRQUAD				/WHERE THIS BELONGS.

WRQUAD,	.-.				/WRITE A 12-BIT WORD TO THE DECTAPE ROUTINE.

/	THE ACTUAL CODE IS IN THE ROM; THIS IS USED ONLY TO ESTABLISH THE PROPER
/	SUBROUTINE LINKAGE AS IT IS CALLED FROM SEVERAL PLACES.

	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO THE ROM FIELD.
	JMP	WRQ			/CONTINUE THERE.

/	COMES BACK HERE FROM THE ROM-BASED CODE THAT CARRIES OUT THE SUBROUTINE CODE.

CIFR,	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO THE ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	WRQUAD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

RDQUAD,	.-.				/READ A 12-BIT WORD FROM THE DECTAPE ROUTINE.
	SDSQ				/QUAD LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, READ IN THE LATEST 12-BIT WORD.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	RDQUAD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

EQUFUN,	.-.				/EQUIVALENCE DATA WITH CHECKSUM ROUTINE.
	CMA				/INVERT THE DATA AND ,XOR. THE DATA WORD.
	DCA	EQUTMP			/SAVE IT.
	TAD	EQUTMP			/GET IT BACK.
	AND	SCKSUM			/GET THE CARRY BITS.
	CIA				/INVERT FOR SUBTRACTION.
	CLL RAL				/MOVE OVER.
	TAD	EQUTMP			/NOW ADD THE LATEST.
	TAD	SCKSUM			/ADD THE NON-CARRY BITS TO RUNNING SUM.
	DCA	SCKSUM			/STORE BACK.
	TAD	EQUTMP			/GET THE LATEST DATA BACK.
	CLL CMA				/NOW HAVE CLEAR LINK AND THE LATEST WORD.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	EQUFUN			/RETURN TO CALLER.

/	THE ROM RETURNS HERE WHEN ALL TAPE OPERATIONS HAVE FINISHED.

	SEXIT=	.			/SUCCESSFUL CALL COMPLETED.

/	TAD	SUNIT			/GET CURRENT TAPE DRIVE UNIT.
/	SDLC				/STOP THE TAPE.

/	OTHER SYSTEM-SPECIFIC OPERATIONS ARE PERFORMED HERE FOLLOWED BY THE RETURN
/	TO THE ORIGINAL CALLER.
/	THE FOLLOWING LOCATIONS MUST BE DEFINED AS REQUIRED BY THE MR8E-C ROM.

